StrataScratch Problem 10090 - "Find the Percentage of Shippable Orders" using Microsoft SQL Server (T-SQL).

SELECT 
    CAST(SUM(CASE WHEN c.address IS NOT NULL THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS FLOAT) 
    / COUNT(*) * 100 AS shippable_percentage
FROM orders o
JOIN customers c
    ON o.cust_id = c.id;

Line-by-line explanation
•	SELECT ... AS shippable_percentage → we're producing one final number, the percentage.
•	SUM(CASE WHEN c.address IS NOT NULL THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) → goes through every order row; if that order's customer has an address, count it as 1, 
  otherwise 0; then adds all these 1s and 0s together. This gives the count of shippable orders.
•	CAST(... AS FLOAT) → converts that count into a decimal number so division doesn't get truncated.
•	/ COUNT(*) → COUNT(*) counts every row (every order) — this is the total number of orders. Dividing shippable/total gives a fraction like 0.75.
•	* 100 → converts the fraction into a percentage, e.g., 0.75 → 75.
•	FROM orders o JOIN customers c ON o.cust_id = c.id → connects each order to its customer's address so we know whether it's shippable.
